# Linear Chains of Magnetic Ions Stacked with Variable Distance: Ferromagnetic Ordering with a Curie Temperature above 20 K

## abstract
We have studied the magnetic properties of the SURMOF-2 series of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). Contrary to bulk MOF-2 crystals, where Cu2  ions form paddlewheels and are antiferromagnetically coupled, in this case the Cu2  ions are connected via carboxylate groups in a zipper-like fashion. This unusual coupling of the spin

The interest in magnetic properties of supramolecular onedimensional (1D) systems[1] does not only originate only from their importance as model systems for the theoretical description of magnetism in confined geometries, but also from the huge potential of such exotic magnetic systems for applications in spintronics and data storage.[2] The first theoretical treatment of magnets arranged in a linear chain was reported by Ising in 1925.[3] His result revealed that in a single, isolated 1D linear chain no magnetic ordering is possible in the ground state. In contrast, for many 2D (and 3D) spin lattices the theoretical analysis reveals a welldefined phase transition to a ferromagnetic state with a transition temperature depending on the magnetic coupling between the individual magnets (see for example, Ref. [4]). Negative magnetic coupling constants $J$ lead to antiferromagnetic (AFM) phases below the Nel temperature, whereas positive $J$ values yield ferromagnetic (FM) ordering below the phase transition at the Curie Temperature $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ .
More recent work, however, has revealed that also 1D systems can show ferromagnetism if the linear (or quasilinear) chains are imbedded in a host matrix providing very small albeit finite magnetic interactions between the chains. The number of materials where such 1D magnetism has been observed experimentally is still rather scarce. For several inorganic compounds, such as phosphates and oxides,[5] for intermetallic compounds, such as $\mathrm{Cr}\bar{\mathrm{S}}\mathbf{b}_{2}^{[6]},$ and also for cobalt chains grown on a $\mathrm{Pt}(111)$ substrate,[7] 1D magnetic signatures have been observed. In addition, for molecular systems, such as stacked metalated porphyrin films, strong antiferromagnetic coupling extending to fairly high temperatures has been reported.[8] A crystalline material which clearly exhibits 1D ferromagnetism at low temperature in the absence of strong external fields and for which $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ can be determined in a straightforward fashion has not yet been reported.
In addition, with the exception of a recently introduced system based on metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[9] the previously reported compounds showing 1D magnetism are hard to produce and difficult to handle[10]—easy-to-fabricate materials suited for applications in spintronics or for data storage have not yet been discovered. MOFs, a class of nanoporous unconventional solids, therefore deserve particular attention.[11] A recent study on the quasi-linear metal-ion chains within a particular type of MOF provided first indications that this easy-to-fabricate class of materials is very interesting in this context. Fe MOF-74 is antiferromagnetic in its ground state but shows ferromagnetic behavior when exposed to high magnetic fields above 2 T,[9b] with a transition temperature of $T_{\mathrm{c}}{\approx}13\mathrm{K}$ . This is an important result, but it has to be noted that such high-field transitions to FM-states are observed for many other materials.[7,12] A 1D (or quasi 1D) arrangement of metal atoms with a $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ above $10\mathrm{K}$ , which shows ferromagnetism in the absence of strong external fields, has not been reported to date.
MOFs are a class of crystalline solids with virtually countless individual members, more than 20 000 of them have been synthesized and characterized in the past 10 years.[13] They have only recently attracted attention with regard to applications as active material in electronic devices.[14] Triggered by the interesting properties of the MOF-74 material we have carried out a more detailed magneticproperty investigation of the isoreticular series SURMOF$2^{[15]}$ since previous work on MOFs containing ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions has revealed interesting magnetic properties[16] of such systems.
The SURMOF-2 series (SURMOF $\c=$ surface attached MOF) can be fabricated in a straightforward fashion using a quasi-epitaxial liquid-phase process[17] (for details of the fabrication process see the Supporting Information) and six different members of this isoreticular series have already been characterized.[15b] We will start our discussion for (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 thin film, a particularly well-studied member of this series (bdc $\c=$ benzdicarboxylic acid).
EPR measurements recorded for the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF2 reveal rather unexpected properties. X-Band EPR data recorded with the magnetic field oriented along the film plane shows an intense signal at a resonance field of $114\mathrm{mT}$ at $5.5\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 1A). Both, the resonance field and the linewidth of this signal, display pronounced dependencies on the temperature (Figure 1 B). Importantly, the inset in Figure 1 B shows a strong variation of the resonance field with the angle $\theta_{\mathrm{F}}$ between the substrate and the magnetic field. The EPR signal disappears at temperatures $T\ge28\:\mathrm{K}$ . In principle, such a signal could be explained by the presence of magnetic species with a high $g$ -value of $g_{j}\approx5.6$ (for more details see the Supporting Information). Although such high $g$ -values have been observed for magnetic 4f and 3d ions with electron spins $S\ge5/2$ , we rule out this explanation since ions carrying such high spins are not present in the MOF thin film. Instead, the precise shape of the EPR signal together with its orientational dependence suggests the presence of a ferromagnetic resonance (FMR) signal.[18] Total magnetization measurements using a SQUID setup provided independent evidence for the presence of ferromagnetism in our samples. Figure 2 shows the magnetization of the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 thin film measured during cooling down from $30\mathrm{K}$ for various magnetic fields with the field vector oriented parallel to the film. The data provide strong evidence for the presence of magnetic order, since the magnetization rises steeply below about $18\mathrm{K}$ and saturates at low temperatures. In large magnetic fields the transition is strongly broadened, a feature commonly observed for ferromagnets with low Curie temperature. The saturation of the magnetization is not due to paramagnetism, since it already occurs in small applied fields $(10\mathrm{mT})$ , see the inset to Figure 2B. The Curie temperature can be determined from the onset of the steep magnetization increase to about $18\mathrm{K}$ .
These experimental results clearly indicate the presence of intrinsic ferromagnetism with a $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ of $18\mathrm{K}$ within (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2. It is not induced by high magnetic fields, in contrast to the previously studied MOF systems.
When carrying out a more detailed theoretical investigation of the magnetic properties exhibited by the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 system we found it impossible to understand the magnetism of the present system on the basis of the previously published structure.[15b] Intact paddlewheel (PW) units (see Figure S12A), composed of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ pairs, are characterized by an antiferromagnetic (AFM) singlet ground state, where the two spins of the adjacent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions couple antiferromagnetically with coupling strength of $38\mathrm{meV}^{[19]}$ or more.[20]
A linear chain formed by such pairs of rather strongly AFcoupled ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions as suggested in previous work[16] thus cannot exhibit a FM phase. Because the SURMOF-2 material can only be grown with thicknesses of up to a few $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , a direct determination of structural parameters using XRD (as employed in a routine fashion for bulk MOF materials)[21] is not possible, the few XRD-peaks determined in the out-ofplane and in-plane scattering geometry only allow the unit cell size to be determined. To overcome this limitation, we have used the liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) process to grow (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 on the surface of small, spherical magnetite particles.[22] The powder XRD-data recorded for these MagMOFs (see Figure S3) contain all possible diffraction peaks (not only the ones perpendicular or parallel to the substrate as in out-of-plane and in-plane XRD data) and allows for a straightforward application of powder-diffraction Rietveld refinement methods. On the basis of these new PXRD data the structural model proposed in the earlier work (linear rows of paired Cu ions) must be rejected. Instead, the Rietveld refinement yielded an unexpected strong deviation of Cu atoms from a straight line (Figure 3). Since the quality of the MagMOF PXRD-data is not sufficient for a determination of the O and C-atoms in the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 compound, we carried out a systematic theoretical re-investigation of the structure of (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 starting from the new experimental data. When carrying out an extensive computational screening of different possible structures for this interesting MOF, including ab initio
Born–Oppenheimer molecular dynamics (MD) and associated simulated annealing calculations, we finally obtained the structure shown in Figure 3. While the position of the Cu atoms is essentially the same as that determined by the Rietveld refinement of the MagMOF PXRD data (see above), interestingly, this structure does not contain intact PW units but instead consists of semi-PWs forming 1D chains of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions linked together in a zipper-like fashion. This novel $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}.$ –acetate structure motive has two $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ distances, a short one of $2.47\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and a longer one of $3.85\mathrm{\AA}$ . This is a very unusual arrangement and has not yet been observed or considered in other ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -carboxylate based MOF types. A motif with remote similarity has been found in copper phosphonates, where a weak ferromagnetism arising from spin canting was observed.[23]
In fact, the structure shown in Figure 3 and Figure S12C fully explains all the spectroscopic and structural data presently available for the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 system (Figure S12B and Figure S10). First, a Rietveld refinement of the MagMOF-particle PXRD data (see above) starting from this new structure yielded only subtle changes of the atomic coordinates (see Supporting Information). Most importantly, electronic structure calculations clearly show a FM coupling between the adjacent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centers. In the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 structure the strong AFM coupling of intact PWs is reversed to a significant FM coupling of $31\mathrm{meV}$ per Cu pair. This is the necessary condition for an FM ordered phase to occur. The other two relevant couplings, that is, the longer distance in the chain and the coupling across the linkers, are small with values beyond the accuracy or DFT calculations (less than $1\mathrm{meV}$ ). Indeed, small, albeit non-zero, coupling constants are fully able to explain the magnetic ordering in the quasi-linear Cu chains. This is demonstrated by the results of a Monte Carlo simulation based on the classical Heisenberg model. When using the values from our DFT calculations (see Figure 4) for the coupling constants, the results of the simulation fully confirm the presence of magnetic ordering. In addition, the Curie temperature derived from these simulations amounts to $18\mathrm{K}$ , in excellent agreement with the values that were found experimentally. Note, that if the coupling between Cu pairs along the chain is antiferromagnetic, as suggested by DFT, at $B=0$ ferromagnetic 2D layers are stacked with weak AFM coupling between them. Extremely low $B$ fields, however, turn that phase into a ferromagnetic one. The experiments carried out at such small fields, $B{=}10~\mathrm{mT}$ , are fully consistent with this finding.
Compared to reported 1D or quasi-1D systems showing signatures of ferromagnetic behavior, the Curie temperature observed here is very large, $T_{\mathrm{c}}{=}18\:\mathrm{K}$ . In addition, the intrinsic ferromagnetism is not field-induced but also observed in the zero-field limit. More importantly, and in contrast to previous reports,[16] a clear orientational dependence of the FMR signal on the direction of the externally applied field is observed. Such an orientational dependence of the FMR signal, a crucial signature for a FM ordering in a thin film, is thus reported for a MOF sample for the first time. In our case the obtained angular dependence of the FMR signal indicates an alignment of the magnetic easy axis parallel to the film plane. In Fe-MOF 74, a previously studied system with a $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ of $13\mathrm{K}$ measured for an external field of $0.1\mathrm{T}.$ , the metal-ion chains are arranged along a helical path with three different, non-collinear ion positions in the unit cell. In the present case of (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2, there are only two magnetic ions per unit cell, and the whole chain is almost perfectly positioned in a plane.
Importantly, the (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 is a member of an isoreticular series of MOFs. The lattice-constant of these molecular solids can be changed easily by using organic struts of different lengths.[15b] As a result of the particular SURMOF-2 structure, changing the linker length does not affect the distance between the Cu ions within the 1D chains (in fact, the geometry stays virtually the same) but changes the distance between the linear chains—an important parameter for the coupling between adjacent chains. To explore the potential of this large flexibility in the geometrical arrangement of the linear magnets, we have studied a second member of the SURMOF-2 class, (Cu,bpdc) SURMOF-2, in which the interchain distance is increased from $10.71\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 to $15.28\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ by using bpdc linkers (bpdc $\c=$ biphenyl-dicarboxylic acid).
(Cu,bpdc) SURMOF-2 shows an equally intense FMR signal with comparable pronounced temperature and angular dependency as the previously presented (Cu,bdc) SURMOF2. An important difference in the EPR results of (Cu,bpdc) is manifested in a different Curie temperature of about $T_{\mathrm{c}}=$ $22\mathrm{K}$ as presented in Figure 5a. Also, the magnetization curves of the (Cu,bpdc) SURMOF-2 are only slightly different from (Cu,bdc) SURMOF-2 (Figure 5b), yet they yield a Curie temperature of $22\mathrm{K}$ with an uncertainty of $0.5{\mathrm{K}}$ in accordance to the FMR data (for more details see Supporting Information).
In conclusion, we have shown that in the recently described, Cu-SURMOF-2 isoreticular series of metal– organic frameworks, the metal ions are arranged in an unusual fashion forming quasi one-dimensional chains. Magnetization measurements reveal that these interesting metal– organic porous solids exhibit a ferromagnetic phase with a Curie temperature of above $20\mathrm{K}$ . The Rietveld refinement of powder X-ray diffraction data, as well as state-of-the-art theoretical studies, reveal that these unexpected properties result from an unusual, zipper-like arrangement of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions. In the future, we plan to explore further members of this highly interesting SURMOF-2 series and in particular to investigate the electronic transport properties.
